0 !ACHILLES {"id":"ACH-Z04-S02","name":"Zone IV · Scene 2: Labors of the Field — The Silent King’s Harvest & Oak Feast","zone":"Z04","lines":"18.550–560","greek":"Ἐν δ᾽ ἐτίθει τέμενος βαθύληϊον· ἔνθα δ᾽ ἔριθοι ἤμων ὀξείας δρεπάνας ἐν χερσὶν ἔχοντες... βασιλεὺς δ᾽ ἐν τοῖσι σιωπῇ σκῆπτρον ἔχων ἑστήκει ἐπ᾽ ὄγμου γηθόσυνος κῆρ. κήρυκες δ᾽ ἀπάνευθε ὑπὸ δρυῒ δαῖτα πένοντο, βοῦν δ᾽ ἱερεύσαντες μέγαν ἄμφεπον· αἱ δὲ γυναῖκες δεῖπνον ἐρίθοισιν λεύκ᾽ ἄλφιτα πολλὰ πάλυνον.","buckley":"There he also placed a field of deep corn, where reapers were reaping, having sharp sickles in their hands. Some handfuls fell thick in series upon the ground along the furrow, and others the binders of sheaves were tying with straw ropes. Three binders followed the reapers, but behind them boys gathering the handfuls, and carrying them in their arms, continually supplied them; and among them the king in silence, over a furrow, was standing at the staff, rejoicing in his heart. But heralds apart, beneath an oak, were preparing a feast, and having sacrificed a huge ox, they were ministering; and women were strewing much white barley meal as a dinner for the reapers.","worldModel":{"vision":"Golden standing grain stalks, silver sickles (37341d), bound sheaves, the purple-and-gold King with his sceptre, and the spreading oak with white barley meal tiles.","memory":"Assembly-line pipeline: reapers cut → handfuls fall → boys gather armfuls → three binders tie sheaves → heralds & women prepare the sacrificial ox and barley feast.","controller":"Ordered reciprocity under the silent, rejoicing gaze of the Basileus."}}

